PLACES=[
N('puukohola-heiau','Puʻukoholā Heiau and Mailekini Heiau','Kawaihae','hi','worship','ruins',20.02751,-155.82149,'site','public','walk','','1790–1791',
  'Puʻukoholā, "temple on the hill of the whale", is the last major temple of old Hawaii. On a priest\'s advice Kamehameha I had it built in less than a year, 1790 to 1791, probably on the site of an older temple of about 1580, with stones passed hand to hand, Wikipedia says, along a human chain from Pololū Valley. When it was finished, about 224 by 100 feet, he invited his cousin and rival Keōua Kuahuʻula to Kawaihae; Keōua and his party were killed as they landed and offered at the new temple. John Young, Kamehameha\'s British adviser, lived nearby and turned the older Mailekini Heiau into a fort guarding the harbour.',
  'The great terraced stone platform on the hill; about 170 feet west of it the ruin of the older Mailekini Heiau; offshore, under water, Hale o Kapuni, a heiau dedicated to sharks, with a stone post on shore that marks where shark feeding was watched. The site of John Young\'s house is inside the park too (Wikipedia). An interpretive trail runs from the visitor center.',
  'The temple that sealed Kamehameha\'s hold on Hawaiʻi Island.',
  'Puʻukoholā Heiau National Historic Site, National Park Service: open daily 8:30 am to 4:30 pm, all year including holidays (NPS hours page, as of November 13, 2025). No entrance pass is needed. Park at the visitor center lot off Spencer Beach Park Road and follow the trail; the heiau is seen from the trail.'+KAPU,
  [['NPS: Puʻukoholā Heiau hours','https://www.nps.gov/puhe/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],['NPS: Puʻukoholā Heiau fees','https://www.nps.gov/puhe/planyourvisit/fees.htm'],W('Puʻukoholā Heiau National Historic Site')],
  th=[20.02651,-155.82009],thn='Visitor center lot, Spencer Beach Park Road',brick='no'),
N('mookini-heiau','Moʻokini Heiau and Kamehameha I birthplace','Hawi','hi','worship','ruins',20.25765,-155.87703,'site','public','drive','','1100–1300?',
  'One of the oldest and most sacred places in Hawaii, a luakini (sacrificial temple) reserved for the highest chiefs. Wikipedia gives the traditions: the chant of its priestly line says the first temple here goes back to the year 480, and the present one is thought to have been built over it by the priest Paʻao some time between 1100 and 1300; one story says the stones came hand to hand from Pololū Valley. Nearby a memorial marks the birthplace of Kamehameha I. Both are in the Kohala Historical Sites State Monument; the heiau is a National Historic Landmark and is still a living temple.',
  'The heiau: an open stone-paved court about 250 by 130 feet inside stone walls that Wikipedia gives as 20 feet high, with the sacrificial stone. The birthplace memorial is a separate walled enclosure nearby. Wikipedia says two smaller heiau near Moʻokini have limited access.',
  'A temple said to be fifteen centuries old at the windy northern tip of the island.',
  'Kohala Historical Sites State Monument, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open daily during daylight hours, no entrance fee; for viewing the heiau and the birthsite. Wikipedia says it is reached only by a dirt road: turn north off ʻAkoni Pule Highway (Route 270) on Upolu Point Road near Hawi, then west at the airstrip, about 1½ miles. The state page names no parking area: park by the site without blocking the road.'+KAPU,
  [SP('Kohala Historical Sites State Monument','kohala-historical-sites-state-monument'),W('Kohala Historical Sites State Monument')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('lapakahi-village','Lapakahi fishing village (Koaiʻe)','Mahukona','hi','town','ruins',20.17527,-155.89768,'area','public','walk','','about 1300–late 1800s',
  'The ruins of Koaiʻe, a coastal fishing village settled around 1300 and lived in until the late 1800s. Its people farmed upslope in an ahupuaʻa that ran from the sea up to Kohala Mountain. It has been excavated and partly restored and is a state historical park and a National Register site (1973).',
  'A self-guided interpretive trail through the dry-laid stone house and burial sites, C-shaped shelters, heiau, salt pans and ovens (Wikipedia). The sea in front is a marine life conservation district.',
  'Walk the lanes of a fishing village lived in for six hundred years.',
  'Lapakahi State Historical Park, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open daily 8:00 am to 3:30 pm, gates lock at 4:00 pm; no entrance fee. The state page says the park reopened on February 29, 2024; its closure notices in November 2026 said it would reopen on Sunday, November 15. Entrance on ʻAkoni Pule Highway (Route 270) north of Kawaihae.',
  [SP('Lapakahi State Historical Park','lapakahi-state-historical-park'),W('Lapakahi State Historical Park')],
  brick='no'),
N('kalahikiola-church','Kalahikiola Church','Kapaau','hi','worship','standing',20.22227,-155.79459,'site','view','drive','','1855–',
  'The mission church of North Kohala, built by hand of stone and mortar and dedicated in 1855 by the missionary Elias Bond after two earlier churches on the site blew down; the wooden tower was added about 1858. Bond went on to found the Kohala Sugar Plantation to support the mission. An earthquake in 2006 brought one wall down; the congregation rebuilt from November 2008, replacing the thick stone walls with concrete block plastered to look like the old irregular stone. The church is part of the Bond District on the National Register (1978), with the Bond homestead and the Kohala Girls\' Seminary uphill.',
  'The church and its square wooden tower at 53-540 Iole Road. The walls seen today are the post-2008 rebuild, not the 1855 stone. Interior not checked. The Bond homestead and seminary buildings are not described as open to visitors in any source read.',
  '',
  'An active church; seen from Iole Road, off ʻAkoni Pule Highway (Route 270) at Kapaʻau. Visiting hours and service times not checked. Do not go onto the Bond homestead land.',
  [W('Bond District')],
  brick='no'),
N('kohala-district-courthouse','Kohala District Courthouse','Kapaau','hi','civic','standing',20.23029,-155.79831,'site','view','drive','','1889–1975',
  'A district court built in 1889 for the Kingdom of Hawaii; it went on serving through annexation and statehood and closed in 1975. It is built like a plantation house, single-walled with a wide lanai and a corrugated metal roof, and its decorated lanai sets it apart from other local buildings. On the National Register since 1979.',
  'The courthouse and, in front of it, a statue of Kamehameha I, who was born in Kohala (Wikipedia). What the building is used for today and whether it is open was not checked.',
  '',
  'On ʻAkoni Pule Highway (Route 270) in Kapaʻau, with roadside parking beside the statue. Seen from the road; inside not checked.',
  [W('Kohala District Courthouse')],
  th=[20.23054,-155.79837],thn='Parking by the Kamehameha statue, ʻAkoni Pule Highway',brick='no'),
N('imiola-church','ʻImiola Church','Waimea','hi','worship','standing',20.0258,-155.66287,'site','view','drive','','1855–',
  'The mission church of Waimea, designed by its first pastor, Lorenzo Lyons, who came in 1832. A stone church with a thatched roof was built here from 1837 to 1843, but by 1855 it was in ruins, and that year a new church of koa wood from the nearby forests was begun. In 1955 the paint was taken off the inside to show the natural wood.',
  'The wooden church; inside, Wikipedia describes the koa woodwork, a hand-carved altar of Hawaiian wood begun by David McHattie Forbes and finished after his death in 1937 by his son, and light fixtures shaped like calabash bowls. Some walls of the old stone church still stand beside it, around the graves of Lorenzo Lyons, his wife and a young son. Whether the church is open outside services was not checked.',
  'A koa-wood church with the walls of its stone predecessor in the churchyard.',
  'An active church on Church Road, Waimea, just off Mamalahoa Highway (Route 19). Opening hours not checked (the church\'s own site did not answer). View from the churchyard and road.',
  [W('ʻImiola Church')],
  brick='no'),
N('mokuaikaua-church','Mokuʻaikaua Church','Kailua-Kona','hi','worship','standing',19.63961,-155.99371,'site','view','walk','','1835–1837',
  'The oldest Christian church in Hawaii. Its congregation was founded in 1820 by Asa and Lucy Thurston from the first company of American missionaries, who landed at Kailua that April. The first churches here, on land given by Governor Kuakini across the street from Huliheʻe Palace, were wood and thatch; after several fires the present stone church was built from about 1835 to 1837, partly of stones taken from a nearby heiau.',
  'The stone church with its tall steeple, long a landmark from the sea, and the gate on Aliʻi Drive built about 1900. Wikipedia says the interior is finished in koa wood and that the church is open for tours, with a scale model of the missionaries\' brig Thaddeus on display; the church\'s own site gives no tour hours.',
  'Hawaii\'s first church, partly built from the stones of a temple.',
  'An active church at 75-5713 Aliʻi Drive, Kailua-Kona; Sunday services, and the office is closed on holidays (church site). Tour hours not checked. Paid lots on Sarona Road are a short walk inland.',
  [['Mokuʻaikaua Church','https://www.mokuaikaua.org/'],W('Mokuʻaikaua Church')],
  th=[19.63882,-155.99229],thn='Lot on Sarona Road',brick='no'),
N('hulihee-palace','Huliheʻe Palace','Kailua-Kona','hi','civic','standing',19.63931,-155.99438,'site','public','walk','','1838–',
  'A lava-rock house built on Kailua Bay by John Adams Kuakini, governor of the island, and later the vacation home of Hawaiian royalty. Princess Ruth Keʻelikōlani made it her main home and invited every reigning monarch from Kamehameha III to Liliʻuokalani; King Kalākaua bought it and in 1885 had the outside plastered. In 1927 the Daughters of Hawaii took it over, saved it from hotel development and restored it as a museum. The 2006 Kīholo Bay earthquake cracked its walls and ceilings.',
  'Six rooms on two floors of koa furniture, feather standards, royal portraits and heirlooms of Princess Ruth, Kalākaua and Kapiʻolani (Daughters of Hawaii); the stone wall and gate on Aliʻi Drive with the royal crest.',
  'A governor\'s lava-rock house that became the royal family\'s seaside retreat.',
  'Daughters of Hawaii museum at 75-5718 Aliʻi Drive: open Wednesday to Saturday, 10 am to 3 pm, last entry 30 minutes before closing. Self-guided entry $16 adult visitors, $14 ages 6 to 17, $12 Hawaii residents, seniors and military, free 5 and under; guided tours ($20) usually Wednesday to Saturday at 11:30 am, space limited. No parking at the palace: free public parking at 75-5668 Kuakini Highway, and paid lots on Sarona Road.',
  [['Daughters of Hawaii: Huliheʻe Palace','https://daughtersofhawaii.org/hulihee-palace'],W('Huliheʻe Palace')],
  th=[19.64088,-155.99444],thn='Free public parking, 75-5668 Kuakini Highway',brick='no'),
N('ahuena-heiau-kamakahonu','Ahuʻena Heiau at Kamakahonu','Kailua-Kona','hi','worship','standing',19.63889,-155.99738,'site','view','walk','','1810–1819',
  'Kamakahonu was the home of Kamehameha I in the last years of his reign, 1810 to 1819, and Ahuʻena was his personal temple. His son Liholiho, Kamehameha II, lived here too; in December 1819 an army left from here to put down the rising that ended at the battle of Kuamoʻo. The first American missionaries landed here on April 4, 1820. The ground is now part of the King Kamehameha\'s Kona Beach Hotel (built 1960, replaced 1974); none of the houses or walls of the royal compound remain.',
  'Ahuʻena Heiau, reconstructed in the 1970s, on its point at the north end of Kailua Bay, with the small beach and the pier. It can be viewed but not entered. Wikipedia says some artifacts, including a feather cloak and helmet, are shown in the hotel lobby.',
  'Where Kamehameha the Great spent his last years.',
  'The heiau stands on the hotel\'s grounds at the north end of Kailua Bay, beside the Kailua pier; view it from the beach and the pier, do not go onto it. The Daughters of Hawaii name free public parking at 75-5668 Kuakini Highway, a few minutes\' walk inland.'+KAPU,
  [W('Kamakahonu')],
  conf='mixed',th=[19.64088,-155.99444],thn='Free public parking, 75-5668 Kuakini Highway',brick='no'),
N('keauhou-holua-slide','Kāneaka (Keauhou) Hōlua Slide','Keauhou','hi','landmark','ruins',19.5615,-155.95511,'site','view','drive','','',
  'The largest hōlua course left in Hawaii. Hōlua was the dangerous sport of racing a narrow sled down a prepared track; Wikipedia says it was practised by men and women of every rank, and that this course was used mostly for ritual. Burials along it suggest that injuries and deaths were common. The course was built of large slabs of pāhoehoe lava topped with smaller and smaller stone and finished with fine ash. It once ran down to the shore at Heʻeia Bay; the part below the road was destroyed for a golf course and vacation homes. A National Historic Landmark.',
  'The broad stone ramp climbing the slope above Aliʻi Drive; short pieces survive on the shoreline where it ended.',
  'A thousand-foot sled run built of lava.',
  'Seen from Aliʻi Drive, across from the Kona Country Club golf clubhouse (Wikipedia); the lot by the clubhouse belongs to the golf club, and no public parking place was found. Who owns the land and whether you may walk onto it was not checked: stay on the road.',
  [W('Kāneaka Hōlua Slide')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('hale-halawai-holualoa','Hale Hālāwai O Hōlualoa (Living Stones Church)','Kailua-Kona','hi','worship','standing',19.60919,-155.97811,'area','view','drive','','1855–',
  'A coral-lime and stone church of about 30 by 60 feet, finished by 1855 under the Rev. John D. Paris on the north end of Hōlualoa Bay; there may have been a wooden church here from about 1825. It was used until the 1940s and was rebuilt in the early 1990s by the Holualoa Bay Congregational Church. Kōnane game boards found in digs here show the site was lived on long before. The land is leased from the state; Living Stones Church holds events on the lawn by the sea.',
  'The rebuilt church, a small graveyard, old stone walls and a canoe landing (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church on Aliʻi Drive south of Kailua-Kona, on state land leased to the congregation. Visiting hours not checked; view from the grounds when open and do not walk among the graves.',
  [W('Hale Halawai O Holualoa')],
  brick='no'),
N('kona-coffee-living-history-farm','Kona Coffee Living History Farm (Uchida Farm)','Captain Cook','hi','industry','standing',19.49074,-155.91388,'site','public','drive','','1900–',
  'A coffee farm begun in 1900 on land of the Greenwell family. Daisaku Uchida, who came from Japan in 1906 on a sugar contract on Kauaʻi, moved here with his wife Shima in 1913 and enlarged the farm and the house in 1925; the family farmed it until 1994. The Kona Historical Society runs it as an open-air museum of a Japanese immigrant coffee family\'s life from 1920 to 1945. On the National Register as the Uchida Coffee Farm.',
  'The farmhouse and the working farm as they were in 1920 to 1945 (Wikipedia). Which original buildings and machinery survive was not checked.',
  'A Japanese immigrant family\'s coffee farm, kept as they left it.',
  'Kona Historical Society, 82-6199 Mamalahoa Highway, Captain Cook; look for the sign on the makai (ocean) side of the highway. Open Tuesdays and Fridays, 10 am to 2 pm, final admission 1:15 pm. Admission $20 adults, $10 ages 7 to 17; Hawaii residents $15 and $5. The society said in November 2026 it must raise $200,000 by December 31, 2026 to stay open to the public: check before going.',
  [['Kona Historical Society: Kona Coffee Living History Farm','https://www.konahistorical.org/kona-coffee-living-history-farm'],W('Kona Coffee Living History Farm')],
  brick='?'),
N('hikiau-heiau','Hikiau Heiau, Kealakekua Bay','Napoopoo','hi','worship','ruins',19.47556,-155.91936,'site','view','drive','','',
  'A luakini temple at the south end of Kealakekua Bay, the old religious centre of Kona and the focus of the Makahiki festival. Captain James Cook, the first documented European to reach Hawaii, recorded the platform as about 16 feet high, 250 long and 100 wide; he was killed across the bay at Kaʻawaloa in 1779. The cliffs above the bay hold royal burials. The bay is a National Register historic district (1973).',
  'The stone platform beside the road at Nāpōʻopoʻo; across the bay at a distance, the Captain Cook monument at Kaʻawaloa. State Parks says the heiau has been restored several times after damage by tsunami and high surf.',
  'The temple where Cook was received, and the shore where he died.',
  'Kealakekua Bay State Historical Park, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open daily 7:00 am to 8:00 pm. The heiau is a sacred site and State Parks asks visitors not to go onto it. To reach the Cook monument at Kaʻawaloa State Parks gives a guided kayak tour with a permitted company or a rented kayak with a valid permit; Wikipedia adds an hour-long hike down a moderately steep trail. State Parks warned in November 2026 that the Nāpōʻopoʻo lot would be closed on November 19 and 20.'+KAPU,
  [SP('Kealakekua Bay State Historical Park','kealakekua-bay-state-historical-park'),W('Kealakekua Bay')],
  th=[19.47375,-155.91923],thn='Kealakekua Bay State Historical Park lot, Beach Road',brick='no'),
N('kahikolu-church','Kahikolu Church','Napoopoo','hi','worship','standing',19.46944,-155.91528,'area','view','drive','','1852–1855',
  'One of only two 19th-century stone churches on Hawaiʻi Island. The Rev. John D. Paris built it from 1852 to 1855 on the ruins of the larger Kealakekua Church of 1839 to 1841, making the old church\'s width the new one\'s length; Kahikolu, "three in one", was the third church on the site. It held about 1,200 people and had a bell given by William E. Dodge of New York. Earthquakes in 1929 and 1951 to 1953 wrecked it and it was abandoned in 1953; a restoration through the 1980s was finished about 1984. In 1993 Henry ʻŌpūkahaʻia, one of the first Native Hawaiian Christians, was reburied here.',
  'The restored stone church with graves on both sides, some unmarked; several bear the name Kamakau. Interior not checked.',
  'A stone church rebuilt from a ruin, where ʻŌpūkahaʻia was brought home.',
  'An active church; address 82-5931 Napoʻopoʻo Road, Captain Cook; Sunday services 9:30 am (Wikipedia). Visiting at other times not checked. Do not walk among the graves.',
  [W('Kahikolu Church')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('st-benedicts-painted-church','St. Benedict\'s Painted Church','Honaunau','hi','worship','standing',19.43471,-155.88806,'site','public','drive','','1899–1902',
  'A small rectangular Catholic church built from 1899 to 1902 under the Belgian missionary Father John Velghe, who then painted its walls and ceiling. Untrained, he painted Bible scenes (Cain and Abel, the temptation of Christ, the writing on the wall, sinners in Hell) and, above the altar, Gothic vaults that make the room look like a European cathedral; Wikipedia says Burgos Cathedral inspired them. Other priests he taught painted more churches, among them the Star of the Sea church at Kalapana.',
  'The painted interior: the false Gothic vaulting and the Bible scenes on the walls; a monument to Father Damien in front.',
  'A one-room church painted inside to look like a Spanish cathedral.',
  'An active parish of the Diocese of Honolulu on Painted Church Road above Hōnaunau, with a small lot beside it. Wikipedia says it is open for viewing seven days a week, with five masses a week; hours not checked (the parish\'s old web address now holds an unrelated site).',
  [W('St. Benedict\'s Catholic Church (Hōnaunau, Hawaii)')],
  th=[19.43461,-155.88828],thn='Church lot, Painted Church Road',brick='?'),
N('puuhonua-o-honaunau','Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau (Place of Refuge)','Honaunau','hi','burial','ruins',19.42163,-155.91209,'site','public','walk','','1475?–1829',
  'Until the early 1800s a Hawaiian who broke a kapu could escape death by reaching this place of refuge and being absolved by a priest; defeated warriors and non-combatants sheltered here in war. Beside it, outside the Great Wall, lived generations of ruling chiefs. Hale o Keawe, built about 1650 by the Kona chief Kanuha for his father Keaweʻīkekahialiʻiokamoku, held the bones of 23 chiefs; the complex may go back to about 1475. It was spared when other temples were destroyed in 1819, but in 1829 Kapiʻolani removed the bones and had it pulled down. Made a national park in 1955.',
  'The Great Wall around the refuge; Hale o Keawe and several thatched houses, reconstructed in the 1960s, with carved wooden images; temple platforms, royal fishponds, hōlua slides and coastal village sites (Wikipedia).',
  'Cross the wall and you were safe.',
  'Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park, National Park Service: gates open daily 8:15 am to sunset. An entrance pass is needed: the standard pass costs $10 to $20 (the NPS page sells it for one private vehicle and its passengers, or for one person on foot or bicycle); cards only, no cash. Park in the lot by the entrance.'+KAPU,
  [['NPS: Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau hours','https://www.nps.gov/puho/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],['NPS: Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau fees','https://www.nps.gov/puho/planyourvisit/fees.htm'],W('Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park')],
  th=[19.42210,-155.91018],thn='Park entrance lot',brick='no'),
N('kaloko-fishpond','Kaloko Fishpond and the Honokōhau settlement','Kailua-Kona','hi','water','ruins',19.68773,-156.03388,'site','public','walk','','',
  'Old Hawaiian fishponds and a coastal settlement on dry lava, now Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park (1978). Kaloko, "the pond", is first named in the story of Kamalālāwalu about 300 years ago; its kuapā (seawall) was built by hand without mortar, its angled, gappy stonework deflecting surf better than many modern seawalls. To the south the Honokōhau settlement, a National Historic Landmark since 1962, has more ponds, house platforms, petroglyphs, a hōlua slide and heiau.',
  'The Kaloko seawall and pond (partly restored); at Honokōhau, the ʻAiʻōpio fishtrap, a stone wall enclosing part of a curved bay, with a heiau on the beach; about a mile of the 19th-century Māmalahoa trail (Wikipedia).',
  'A mortarless seawall that has outlasted concrete.',
  'Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park, National Park Service: visitor center (Hale Hoʻokipa, 74-4968 Queen Kaʻahumanu Highway) and its lot open daily 8:30 am to 4:00 pm, cars out by 4:00 pm; the Kaloko Road gate, which leads to the Kaloko pond lot, is open 8:00 am to 5:00 pm. For ʻAiʻōpio, park by the Kona Sailing Club at Honokōhau Harbor and walk to the beach; walkers may stay after 4:00 pm. No camping; dogs on a 6-foot leash.',
  [['NPS: Kaloko-Honokōhau hours','https://www.nps.gov/kaho/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],W('Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park')],
  th=[19.68690,-156.03245],thn='Kaloko pond lot at the end of Kaloko Road',brick='no'),
]
